  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge continues to sit over SD today and into \nMonday, with multiple rounds of shortwave moving over the state \nduring that time. At the same time, a surface trough continues to \nsit over western SD with an attached stationary front stretching from \nnorthwest to southeast SD. Scattered showers and storms look to \ncontinue to develop as the shortwaves move over the boundaries this \nmorning through Monday morning. Some models are hinting at showers \nand storms developing early this morning from a low level jet, which \nthen move into northeastern SD where they continue to stay into the \nearly afternoon. CAPE values in northeastern SD this morning into \nthe afternoon will be between 1000-3000 J/kg, which will help with \nthe continue development over the area as the low level jet and \nshortwaves provide some upper-level support. With very little shear \nover northeastern SD, the storms will have a hard time becoming \nsupercellular and severe. However, with stronger lapse rates, they \ncould develop hail and some strong winds. There is a marginal risk \n(level 1 out of 5) for these storms to become severe, and if they \ndo, the wind gusts could get up to 60 mph and hail up around 1 inch \nin diameter.\n\nThe warm, moist air moving in today will help to support the storms \nthis morning and early afternoon, as well as helping to warm the \ntemperatures at the surface and aloft. 850 temperatures this \nafternoon and evening will be between 20-24 degrees C, which will \nhelp to place a decent cap over central and northeastern SD. This \ncap will help the early afternoon storms to taper off and keep \nstrong storms from developing late this afternoon and into the \nevening, though there could be some weak scattered pulsy showers and \nstorms that develop and try to break through the cap. As \ntemperatures cool slightly overnight into Monday morning weakening \nthe cap, as well as another nocturnal low level jet developing over \ncentral and northeastern SD, some more isolated showers and storms \ncould try to develop overnight into Monday morning. While there will \nbe some instability to help them develop, the stronger lapse rates \nand shear will not be there to help them become severe. \n\nAn upper-level low and trough over the Pacific Northwest will be \npushing east Monday and Tuesday, which will finally cause the ridge \nto move out of the state Monday night. This upper-level trough will \nbe supporting a cold front pushing through SD Monday night. This \nfront will be moving into an environment that has had warm, moist \nair being pushed into it through the day. The NAM shows CAPE values \nof 1000-3000 J/kg building over central and northeastern SD through \nthe day. Storms will be able to develop as the front moves through \nlate Monday afternoon into the nighttime hours. Lapse rate values of \n5-7 C/km as well as shear values of 30-40 kts could help hail and \nstrong winds to occur in the storms that do develop. There is a \nmarginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for storms to become severe, with \nthe primary threats of 60 mph wind gusts and hail up around 1 inch \nin diameter. \n\nEarly Monday morning and through the day, as the front is \ndeveloping, the pressure gradient over SD strengthens. This will \ncause the winds to strengthen aloft and get to the surface as the \nlapse rates start to pull the winds down during the day. Winds will \npick up during the afternoon, with models showing a 30-50% chance \nfor gusts to be above 40 mph Monday afternoon over portions of \nnortheastern SD. The winds look to pick up again Tuesday over north \ncentral SD with a 40-60% chance for gusts the get above 45 mph. \nThese winds, as well as the relative humidity, will need to be \nwatched over the coming days to see if the models continue to show \nstrong winds and lower humidity. If it does, there could be some \nwind and fire weather concerns. Temperatures behind the front will \ncool to be around normal for this time of the year Tuesday and \nWednesday. Highs Tuesday look to be in the 80s and highs Wednesday \nin the 70s.",
